---
import { type GameDefinition } from '@/games/registry';
import GameLayout from '@/layouts/GameLayout.astro';

import LinkUp from './components/LinkUp';

interface Props {
  game: GameDefinition;
}

const { game } = Astro.props;
---

<GameLayout game={game}>
  {/* Starts the first puzzle (and fetches its words) while the game is still loading */}
  <script>
    import { firstGame } from './logic/first-game';

    firstGame().catch(() => {
      // The game shows the error itself
    });
  </script>
  {/* Rendered in the browser only: the puzzle is random */}
  <LinkUp client:only="react">
    <p
      slot="fallback"
      class="grid min-h-[38.8rem] place-items-center text-muted [@media(max-width:30rem)]:min-h-[39.8rem]"
    >
      Loading the game...
    </p>
  </LinkUp>
  <noscript>
    <p class="text-muted">
      This game needs JavaScript to run. Nothing is sent anywhere, it all happens in your browser.
    </p>
  </noscript>

  <div slot="rules">
    <p>
      Sixteen terms belong to <strong>four hidden groups of four</strong>. Pick four terms that you
      think belong together. When the fourth one is picked, the group is checked by itself.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Right:</strong> the four tiles light up in a color, glide to the top and show the
        name of the group. The other tiles move up.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Wrong:</strong> the tiles shake, and you lose one of your four tries. The same wrong
        group again costs nothing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>One away:</strong> three of your four tiles belong together. You get a hint, and it
        still counts as a mistake.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Out of tries:</strong> the missing groups are shown one after the other.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Not sure what a term means? Point at it, or focus it with the keyboard: a line under the board
      says what it is. The eye next to that line blurs the definitions if you want a harder game.
      You can do that for the terms of the groups you found, too. A puzzle is made from over 200
      topics (sorting algorithms, HTTP methods, Git commands, Linux distributions, fonts, optimizers
      and many more), and only terms that clearly belong to one topic are mixed, so every puzzle has
      exactly one solution.
    </p>
    <p>
      Rounds are unlimited and nothing is saved. The link of a puzzle opens the same puzzle for
      whoever you send it to. You can also add <code>?seed=anything</code> to the address.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div slot="keys">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <kbd>Tab</kbd> moves into the board, then the arrow keys <kbd>←</kbd> <kbd>↑</kbd>
        <kbd>→</kbd> <kbd>↓</kbd> move between the tiles
      </li>
      <li>
        <kbd>Space</kbd> or <kbd>Enter</kbd> picks a tile (or puts it back)
      </li>
      <li>
        <kbd>Home</kbd> and <kbd>End</kbd> jump to the first and the last tile
      </li>
      <li>
        <kbd>Esc</kbd> puts all picked tiles back
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The fourth picked tile hands the group in by itself. Everything that happens is announced to
      screen readers.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div slot="built">
    <p>
      The rules are small, pure TypeScript functions with unit tests: making a puzzle from random
      numbers, counting the solutions of a puzzle, and the game as a value that is never changed
      (picking, handing in, mistakes, losing and showing the missing groups). The user interface is
      a React component that only draws what the rules say.
    </p>
    <p>
      The topics, the terms and their short definitions are written for this site. A puzzle is only
      shown after it was checked: a separate function counts every way to sort the sixteen tiles
      into the four groups, and it must find exactly one. Every term belongs to exactly one topic,
      and a list of topics that must never meet (HTTP DELETE is also an SQL command) keeps the
      answer clear. Tests check every term (a short definition that does not give the term away, no
      duplicates) and generate hundreds of puzzles. The animations are CSS, plus a short slide of
      the tiles that is measured and played in the browser, and they all stop when your system asks
      for reduced motion. Nothing is stored on your device.
    </p>
  </div>
</GameLayout>
